Sukun Tarachandani: And, like, if you read the gossip-sub specs, the aim was that it should be a baseline protocol for PubSub, on which you can build your own nicer PubSub protocol, more fancier protocol. But the problem was that it either did not offer the correct primitives, like topic streams, or…

194
00:21:24.460 --> 00:21:37.740
Sukun Tarachandani: as we saw partial messages, or it offered some primitives which were just not working, at least for Ethereum, like a message ID, which is very closely tied to the set of bytes and, like, the hash of it.

195
00:21:38.220 --> 00:21:49.539
Sukun Tarachandani: like, the scene cache and everything, which makes things very difficult to override. And, so, we have started moving away from these sorts of limitations by partial messages and topic streams.

196
00:21:49.680 --> 00:21:55.480
Sukun Tarachandani: And the idea is that, okay, at this point, yeah, basically we start with a clean slate and put

197
00:21:55.860 --> 00:22:01.269
Sukun Tarachandani: Roughly have a protocol which is equivalent to partial messages and topic streams, but without the burden of…

198
00:22:01.530 --> 00:22:05.450
Sukun Tarachandani: One backwards compatibility, and two…

199
00:22:05.830 --> 00:22:25.930
Sukun Tarachandani: some of the other things which we felt went wrong with gossip-sub, so I'll just very QUICly go through them. Yeah, so one thing, just at the start, this is, like, very orthogonal to what your connection to the peer is. Like, it really does not matter if your connection is a QUIC connection, TCP connection, carrier pigeons, multi-stream select, edge 3 streams.

200
00:22:25.930 --> 00:22:31.780
Sukun Tarachandani: Like, whatever you give, like, a broadcast protocol can work with any connection, right? Yeah.

201
00:22:31.780 --> 00:22:42.219
Sukun Tarachandani: So, like, we can run this on lib P2P, and then when we get ETH P2P with a fancier protocol select, we can just move to those connections. Like, this protocol has nothing to do with

202
00:22:42.230 --> 00:22:45.639
Sukun Tarachandani: The transport that the peer is talking to us on.

203
00:22:47.260 --> 00:22:57.919
Sukun Tarachandani: So, some things that we want to, like, have in Waggle and, like, fix in Waggle, like, the details of the spec car and the specs link.

204
00:22:58.560 --> 00:23:09.019
Sukun Tarachandani: please, like, engage there, and we will keep on, like, iterating. These are very… this is a very early spec, we'll keep on iterating, but yeah, that's the place for discussion.

205
00:23:09.020 --> 00:23:26.739
Sukun Tarachandani: The other thing I'd like to also say is that we can move this spec discussion to data P2P if people want to, like, break away from lib P2P sort of thing. Yeah. So QUICly, basically, yeah, the idea is message semantics should be in the application, like, think of aggregates, I feel that's, like, the best example.

206
00:23:26.800 --> 00:23:42.789
Sukun Tarachandani: Currently, there are 16 aggregators who send only 2 or 3 distinct aggregates among them. But for gossip sub, these are 16 distinct messages, and there is no easy way to hack the message ID function to make them as 2 or 3 distinct messages only.

207
00:23:42.890 --> 00:23:51.390
Sukun Tarachandani: You will end up sending all the 16 distinct messages, because on the wire, they are distinct, as the selection proof of the aggregators is distinct.

208
00:23:52.300 --> 00:24:08.860
Sukun Tarachandani: The other part is peer scoring. This is also something that application needs to control, because different applications have different requirements, and in general, application also wants to do a lot of cross-topic peer scoring, basically.

209
00:24:09.030 --> 00:24:23.419
Sukun Tarachandani: So, like, yeah, it's roughly… this should be in the application. To the question that, are we losing out on the fact that if gossip-sub has this handcrafted application scoring logic over theirs.

210
00:24:23.420 --> 00:24:38.170
Sukun Tarachandani: My opinion of it is that if that logic is so important, we should provide it as a library, not as a framework that you opt into. So it should be, like, a library that you can use if you want, otherwise, like, discard, but the ownership of the thing should be completely in the application.

211
00:24:38.240 --> 00:24:44.589
Sukun Tarachandani: More contentious, consider removing a fixed mesh, and just forward to a random peerset.

212
00:24:44.790 --> 00:24:53.770
Sukun Tarachandani: I do think we can make this work, like, this is what EC-broadcast also does, it has no fixed peer set, and peers just randomly push.

213
00:24:54.470 --> 00:25:05.530
Sukun Tarachandani: The only problem is that you need to ensure that you have a sufficient set of peers, so that you are eventually picked by that peer's random broadcasting logic.

214
00:25:06.450 --> 00:25:17.700
Sukun Tarachandani: Yeah, basically, yeah, the biggest reason why we are doing this, because, like, the performance, I expect, will be equivalent to this sort of thing, with topic streams plus partial messages that gossip gives us.

